From: build fix
Subject: [PATCH] uk_interface: define compat types when !CONFIG_COMPAT

compat_uptr_t and compat_ulong_t come from <linux/compat.h>, which is only
built when CONFIG_COMPAT is set -- i.e. a 64-bit kernel running 32-bit
userspace. On a 32-bit ARM kernel CONFIG_COMPAT is off and neither type
exists, but sw_api_ks_ioctl.c still needs them to compile:

  - input_parser()/output_parser()/sw_api_cmd() all take compat_ulong_t *
  - the ternaries at lines 133/146/172/199 reference compat_args[] and
    compat_ptr(), dead on this path but still parsed
  - the !CONFIG_COMPAT compat_ptr() stub itself takes a compat_uptr_t

12.5.0.6 supplies the stub function but not the types, so the build dies with
"unknown type name 'compat_uptr_t'". Only 64-bit configurations are exercised
by the vendor, which is why this went unnoticed.

Both types are u32 on every arch that defines them, and the compat ioctl path
(switch_compat_ioctl) stays compiled out, so this is compile-only.

--- a/src/sal/sd/linux/uk_interface/sw_api_ks_ioctl.c
+++ b/src/sal/sd/linux/uk_interface/sw_api_ks_ioctl.c
@@ -96,6 +96,9 @@
 };
 
 #ifndef CONFIG_COMPAT
+typedef u32 compat_uptr_t;
+typedef u32 compat_ulong_t;
+
 #if (LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 1, 0))
 static inline void __user *compat_ptr(compat_uptr_t uptr)
 {
